<!--
  On-screen keyboard height for our short screen.

  The screen is 1080 x 1240 px. At TARGET_SCREEN_DENSITY 380 that is 455 x 522 dp, and the app
  keeps 496 dp of it once the status bar is gone  - roughly half the vertical room a normal
  phone has, which is why a keyboard sized for a normal phone swallows this screen.

  Stock LatinIME asks for 205.6 dp. The previous value here was 150 dp, chosen when the tree
  still set density 360; at 380 that works out to a 37 dp key row, below the 48 dp touch target
  and noticeably cramped  - "the standard keyboard was smaller" is exactly that.

  210 dp gives a ~52 dp key row, comfortably above the target, and with the suggestion strip on
  top the keyboard lands near half the usable height. That is the shape asked for: half the
  screen, and keys big enough to hit.

  🔴 Two things outside this file decide how much of that height is actually keys:
    - the framework dimens overlay sets navigation_bar_frame_height to 0, so no system inset is
      wasted under the bottom row;
    - config.xml here zeroes LatinIME's own bottom padding.
  Without both, height added here goes into empty space instead of into keys.

  🔴 This file governs the AOSP keyboard only. Gboard is a separate application and ignores it;
  its height and its toolbar row are its own settings.

  Why config_default_keyboard_height and not the keyboard-heights.xml table: that table is keyed
  by Build.HARDWARE and ours (mt8781) is not in it, so the device already falls back to this
  default  - this is the value that actually applies.
-->
<resources>
    <dimen name="config_default_keyboard_height">210dp</dimen>
</resources>
